1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,967
When the climate shifts, credit risk shifts with it.

2
00:00:03,967 --> 00:00:09,859
This is ClimaRisk Tunisia: a map of the physical climate risk inside a bank loan portfolio.

3
00:00:15,599 --> 00:00:22,097
The floods in Nabeul in 2018, droughts that hit the cereal harvest, record heat.

4
00:00:22,097 --> 00:00:26,239
The assets that Tunisian banks finance are exposed.

5
00:00:26,239 --> 00:00:37,448
In November 2026, according to the Tunisian press, the central bank launched a roadmap on green finance and climate risk for 2026 to 2030.

6
00:00:37,448 --> 00:00:42,158
This project sits where banking risk meets climate change.

7
00:00:43,758 --> 00:01:06,052
The rules have been built step by step: the Basel principles in 2022, the IFRS S2 standard in 2023, phase five of the NGFS scenarios in 2024, the European Banking Authority guidelines and the voluntary Basel disclosure framework in 2025, then the Tunisian roadmap.

8
00:01:06,052 --> 00:01:08,501
The tool helps banks prepare.

9
00:01:08,501 --> 00:01:10,950
It does not claim compliance.

10
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:20,387
The data are fully open: NASA climate projections, WRI flood maps, ESA land cover and WorldPop population.

11
00:01:20,387 --> 00:01:31,552
For each loan, the method combines hazard, exposure and the vulnerability of its sector, then adjusts the probability of default and the expected loss.

12
00:01:31,552 --> 00:01:35,840
The portfolio of two thousand loans is entirely synthetic.

13
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:48,837
The result: the share of the loan book in a high-hazard zone rises from 1.5 percent today to 22.4 percent in 2050, under the high-emissions scenario.

14
00:01:48,837 --> 00:01:57,321
The extra expected loss reaches 8.8 million dinars, or 29 basis points of the book, 4.8 million more than today.

15
00:01:57,321 --> 00:02:00,730
These figures describe a synthetic portfolio.

16
00:02:00,730 --> 00:02:08,381
Cereals and olives are the most affected sectors, and the spread between climate models remains wide.

17
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:26,244
The website tells the story on a single map, then lets you switch scenarios, explore the governorates and sectors, and download the working paper and the data.

18
00:02:42,550 --> 00:02:48,012
These figures are illustrative and must be calibrated on real bank data.

19
00:02:48,012 --> 00:02:53,701
ClimaRisk Tunisia, an open-source project by Emna Saada, in geoinformatics.
